From bf2360e7eef4d6a6f6497949db0d48aa5b5d59ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: halt95 <halt95@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 21:16:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 65/76] rc3-instr 9/9: bound the RoPE position before it
 becomes an address

The guard the step-3 run argued for, and the only one in this series that
covers that chain.

STEP3-RESULTS.md adjudicated the R-ring finding: the QSA pre-indexer takes the
RoPE position for a cross-chunk pooled group out of the raw-key RING row
(qsa_pre_indexer.py:305-317) and _norm_rope turns it straight into a row index
into the cos/sin table (:44-51). The masks there bound which rotary PAIR is
read, never which row, so `cos_sin_ptr + pos * cos_sin_stride` is an address
formed from an unchecked value. state_block_valid (:236) bounds the ring BLOCK
ID, not its content -- and the ring is excluded from zeroing
(single_type_kv_cache_manager.py:96-101), so a re-claimed block keeps the
previous occupant's bytes. Worse, nothing ties the row the reader selects
(`first_position % STATE_SIZE`) to a row any occupant ever committed, so it can
be allocator residue rather than a stale position. Compute-sanitizer reported
eleven invalid 2-byte reads at +4.6e18 bytes on that case.

Commit 4 bounds the pre-indexer's `request`. It sits upstream of the ring read
and cannot see a bad position, so it would not have masked this. Nothing else
in the series touches it.

The guard lives inside _norm_rope rather than at the call site, so it covers
BOTH position sources with one check: the pooled path above and the q/k path at
:185, whose positions come from the host. It clamps rather than skips -- the
rotation is then wrong for those rows, which is what a masked fault IS; the
counter is the result, not the output. Recorded at a new telemetry site,
SITE_QSA_ROPE_POS, with the worst offending position in slot 1 and the bound it
broke in slot 2.

Commits 1-8 are unchanged; this adds the site constant to guard_telemetry and
one new kernel argument.

Tests, tests/v1/worker/gpu/test_qsa_rope_position_guard.py:

  * CPU -- the launcher passes the cos/sin table's real row count, and the
    counter it passes is the shared persistent slice rather than a fresh
    tensor. This is the failure mode a CPU suite can catch: a bound that is too
    large lets through exactly the reads the guard exists to stop, and one that
    is too small makes it fire on correct metadata, which is worse than having
    no guard at all.
  * GPU (gated) -- the positive control, which is the step-3 case verbatim:
    fill a re-claimed ring row with bf16 filler, run the kernel, require that
    it SURVIVES and that the counter is non-zero and recorded an out-of-range
    position. The same input faulted the SM before this commit. It asserts the
    count, not merely the survival, because a silent counter would mean the
    guard is not on the path -- and a zero counter would then be read as clean.

CPU suite: 18 passed / 50 skipped (was 17 / 49 at commit 8; +1 passed, +1
CUDA-gated).

Not compiled, not executed on a GPU.

ROUND 5 (review R3-1, blocking, and R3-2).

R3-1. The site was declared, named, documented -- and never written. Both
_norm_rope calls passed commit 4's guard_ctr, i.e.
counter(SITE_QSA_PRE_INDEXER), so SITE_QSA_ROPE_POS would have read 0 for the
life of every run and been reported as "the rope chain is clean", which is the
exact misreading this series exists to prevent; rope hits would have landed in
the pre-indexer's request-row slot; and the GPU positive control, which reads
site 9, could not have passed while sending the investigator somewhere else.
Worst of all the CPU test asserted the wrong pointer, so the suite was green
BECAUSE the wiring was wrong. There is now a distinct rope_guard_ctr parameter
fed by counter(SITE_QSA_ROPE_POS), and the CPU test asserts the two counters
DIFFER.

R3-2. Slot 1 recorded pos_t whichever axis offended. Under the production mrope
section (11, 11, 10) all three components are live row indices -- the IS_MROPE
branch masks COLUMNS per axis while the row comes unmasked from pos_rows[axis]
-- so a pos_w-only fault recorded an in-range value next to a non-zero count.
The record is now the worst offending value ACROSS t/h/w with the axis in slot
2 (0=t, 1=h, 2=w), and the tl.where filler is strongly negative rather than 0,
so an all-negative offender is not lost to the filler (B4 at the one site with
a reproduced fault). A parametrised GPU control covers a single bad axis for
each of t, h and w, positive and negative.

The site is registered in _ALWAYS_FATAL_SITES: a clamped rope position is not a
locally wrong answer that the next step forgets. On the pooled path the
wrongly-rotated key is STORED into the compressed cache, so every later step
reads it and the ring row that produced it is still there to do it again.
Continuing past this one collects evidence about corrupted state, so it aborts
the stage even in MODE=warn.

ROUND 6 (review R4-1). The axis in slot 2 is now derived from the MASKS --
any_t, then any_h, in t/h/w order -- not by comparing the winning value against
each axis's maximum. Value comparison mis-attributes whenever two axes share
the worst value, which is the common case rather than a corner: pos_h and pos_w
are frequently equal to pos_t (the non-2D path sets them equal, and a stale
ring tail writes the same int64 into all three), so the round-5 version would
have reported axis 0 for a genuine pos_w fault.

_FILL drops from -(1<<30) to -(1<<62), comfortably below any int64 position a
corrupted ring row can produce, so the filler cannot win the max and be read
back as an offending value.

The per-axis control gains a negative_only parametrisation. All-negative
offenders are the case the filler decides, and the case the original 0 filler
got wrong; mixed-sign seeding would have passed either way.

Draft for Astra review. Not merged into any RC.
